--- Mrs. Ada Turner's diary, Tuesday, the 6th of January, 1931 (3/3 key facts kept) ---
To-day I pawned Bert's Sunday suit for five shillings, to get bread. I redeemed Fred's watch from the pawnbroker for eleven shillings, to buy coal. I pawned Fred's silver watch again for ten shillings, to pay the rent. I spoke to the constable, and asked to pledge Fred's watch once more, but he refused me.

--- Mr. Arthur Hale's diary, Tuesday, the 6th of January, 1931 (0/0 key facts kept) ---
1. Read in the Daily Herald of the death of Princess Louise.

--- Mr. Walter Thomas's diary, Tuesday, the 6th of January, 1931 (0/0 key facts kept) ---
1. Read in The Times that all Britain mourned Louise, Princess Royal, nicknamed “Her Royal Shyness,” who died yesterday of heart disease.
   ☾ Ada: She dreamt that Fred was dead, and that she and Bert were following his body to the grave.
      📖 It foretells death.
   ☾ Arthur (forgotten by morning): He dreamt that he was walking up Ludgate Hill with a red flag in his hand, and that all the people cheered him.
   ☾ Mr. Thomas: He dreamt that he was reading Trollope in a railway carriage, and that, looking out of the window, he saw a great plain covered with snow, and people skating busily to and fro upon the ice.

